---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import About from "../components/About.astro";
import sweepFig from "../figures/taiwan_sweep.png";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
const sections = [
  { id: "the-fault-and-the-mesh", label: "The fault and the mesh" },
  { id: "the-tensors", label: "The tensors" },
  { id: "what-is-solved", label: "What is solved" },
  { id: "what-the-sweep-says", label: "What the sweep says" },
  { id: "what-this-is-not", label: "What this is not" },
  { id: "reproducing-it", label: "Reproducing it" },
];
---
<Base
  title="mhs — Taiwan"
  description="The Longitudinal Valley Fault of eastern Taiwan, from two Taiwan Earthquake Model segments, under a sweep of regional stress tensors: the static Coulomb LCP's slip, activation load and rake on every element, for the mollified half-space kernel and the classical dislocation, two effective-stress models, and loads to six times the first failure."
  current="taiwan"
>
  <Fragment slot="sidebar">
    <h1>Taiwan<span class="sub">the Longitudinal Valley Fault under a sweep of stress tensors</span></h1>
    <p>
      One real fault instead of a synthetic one. Two Taiwan Earthquake Model
      segments of the Longitudinal Valley Fault, 2,498 triangles striking
      N20E and dipping about 60&deg; east to 20&nbsp;km, breaking the surface
      along 147&nbsp;km of trace, loaded by a regional stress whose shape and
      orientation are swept: the most compressive axis at seven azimuths
      from 90&deg; to 150&deg;, and a regime from pure thrust to pure
      strike-slip in five steps.
    </p>
    <p>
      At each tensor the static Coulomb LCP is solved along the load, from
      the first failure to six times it. What is painted is one value per
      element: the slip at a chosen load, the load at which the element first
      slipped, the rake the tensor imposes, or how fast the tensor loads the
      element toward failure. Two effective-normal-stress models and two
      kernels, so that neither the strength profile nor the kernel can be
      mistaken for the result.
    </p>
    <nav class="toc">
      <h3>Contents</h3>
      <ol>{sections.map((s) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.label}</a></li>)}</ol>
    </nav>
    <hr />
    <p class="contact">
      Produced by <code>studies/active_fractal/taiwan.py</code> and gated by
      <code>selfcheck.py -k taiwan</code>. The segments are the user&rsquo;s
      meshes of the Taiwan Earthquake Model seismogenic structures, used as
      supplied.
    </p>
    <About />
  </Fragment>

  <div id="viewer" class="onfault">
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="theta"><span>S<sub>Hmax</sub> azimuth</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="r"><span>regime r</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="model"><span>effective stress</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="kernel"><span>kernel</span></label>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="metric"><span>metric</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="load"><span>load</span></label>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="window"><span class="lbl">window</span></div>
    </div>
    <div data-canvas class="canvas" aria-label="The two segments of the Longitudinal Valley Fault, coloured by the static LCP's slip, activation load, rake or Coulomb loading rate, one value per element"></div>
    <p data-legend class="legend"></p>
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="mark"><span>mark saturated</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="wire"><span>element edges</span></label>
      <span class="lbl">view</span>
      <span class="btns" data-ctl="view"></span>
      <span class="btns" data-ctl="shot"></span>
      <label data-ctl="publication"><span>publication view</span></label>
    </div>
    <p data-status class="status">loading&hellip;</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="the-fault-and-the-mesh">The fault and the mesh</h2>
  <p>
    The Longitudinal Valley Fault is the suture between the Philippine Sea
    plate and Eurasia in eastern Taiwan, an east-dipping reverse fault with
    a left-lateral component that creeps along part of its length and
    broke in 1951 and 2003. The two segments here are its southern 88&nbsp;km
    and northern 58&nbsp;km, meeting at one trace vertex, which is the origin
    of the local frame: x east, y north, z up, kilometres, the surface at
    z&nbsp;=&nbsp;0. Geographic azimuths are clockwise from north, so the
    fault&rsquo;s normal points to about 110&deg;.
  </p>
  <p>
    The meshes are used as supplied: 1,499 and 999 triangles with a median
    edge of 1.9&nbsp;km, a smallest edge of 0.12&nbsp;km and two near-vertical
    slivers at the bottom and the top. Every triangle is wound so that its
    normal points up, cutde&rsquo;s frame is checked against the
    package&rsquo;s on every facet, the tiling is checked to be a manifold
    with one boundary loop per segment, and the reader is checked against
    meshio bit for bit. The mollification width is a tenth of the median
    edge, 0.19&nbsp;km, and the free surface is folded into the kernel as on
    the <a href={`${base}onfault/`}>on-fault page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-tensors">The tensors</h2>
  <p>
    The load is a unit deviator times &lambda;, the maximum shear stress the
    regional field carries, plus an isotropic part of two thirds of &lambda;,
    on an effective normal stress of 150&nbsp;MPa everywhere or lithostatic
    minus a pore-pressure ratio of 0.4. The deviator&rsquo;s most compressive
    axis is horizontal at azimuth &theta;, and its shape is set by a regime
    parameter r: at r&nbsp;=&nbsp;0 the vertical stress is the least
    compressive (the thrust regime), at r&nbsp;=&nbsp;1 it is the
    intermediate one (strike-slip), and in between the principal values are
    (&minus;1, r, 1&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;r) scaled to unit maximum shear, so that
    &lambda; means the same thing on the whole family and
    r&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.5 is the axially symmetric compression where the two
    regimes are equally favoured. Friction is 0.6 everywhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tensor fixes each element&rsquo;s slip direction: the rake is the
    direction of the resolved shear, and the LCP solves only for its
    magnitude. The rake map is therefore a prediction of the tensor and not
    of the solve. With the compressive axis at 110&deg;, normal to the fault,
    every element slips reverse; at 90&deg; and 100&deg; a right-lateral
    component appears, and from 120&deg; on a left-lateral one, which is the
    sense observed on the fault.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-is-solved">What is solved</h2>
  <p>
    The static LCP of the <a href={`${base}avalanche/`}>avalanche page</a>:
    one strength per element, slip wherever the shear reaches it and nowhere
    else, one unique answer at each load, no events. The load is scanned from
    &lambda;<sub>0</sub>, the load at which the best-oriented element reaches
    its strength with no interaction under the uniform model, to
    6&nbsp;&lambda;<sub>0</sub>, warm-started, and the lithostatic model is
    scanned at the same absolute loads, so the two models are compared under
    one tectonic stress. The quantities quoted below are in MPa of maximum
    shear, which is what makes tensors of different shape comparable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two things the linear Coulomb criterion cannot say are stated beside
    the maps instead. An element whose normal stress the tensor raises faster
    than its shear can never fail at any load, and the legend gives that area
    for the chosen tensor. And an element whose effective normal stress the
    load drives through zero would open rather than slip; the manifest
    records the area in that state at every load; it stays below 1.2&nbsp;%
    of the fault on either model within the scan.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-the-sweep-says">What the sweep says</h2>
  <figure class="panels">
    <img src={sweepFig.src} alt="Six panels: first-failure load, half-active load for both stress models, area-mean rake, never-failing area, and activation curves at 130 degrees" />
    <figcaption>
      <b>The sweep in six numbers.</b> <b>a</b> the first-failure load
      &lambda;<sub>0</sub> against the azimuth of the most compressive axis,
      one line per regime. <b>b</b>, <b>c</b> the load at which half the
      fault&rsquo;s area is slipping, uniform and lithostatic effective
      stress, solid for the mollified kernel and dashed for the classical
      dislocation. <b>d</b> the area-mean rake the tensor imposes.
      <b>e</b> the area the tensor can never bring to failure. <b>f</b> the
      active area against the load at 130&deg;, solid uniform, dotted
      lithostatic. A missing point in b or c is a tensor that does not bring
      half the area to failure by 6&nbsp;&lambda;<sub>0</sub>.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <b>The first failure says nothing about the tensor.</b> &lambda;<sub>0</sub>
    is 117 to 159&nbsp;MPa and flat in azimuth (panel a): it is set by
    whichever sliver of the supplied mesh happens to be best oriented, and
    it moves with the regime only because the family&rsquo;s intermediate
    stress changes the normal stress on that sliver. The load at which half
    the fault is slipping (b, c) is the number that discriminates.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Fault-normal compression is the hardest way to load this fault.</b>
    With the compressive axis at 110&deg;, normal to the strike, the thrust
    tensor needs 478&nbsp;MPa of maximum shear to bring half the area to
    failure, against 387&nbsp;MPa at 130&deg; and 316&nbsp;MPa at 150&deg;:
    a 60&deg; fault is poorly oriented for pure thrust, and obliquity helps.
    For the strike-slip half of the family the fault-normal case does not
    activate at all: with r&nbsp;&ge;&nbsp;0.5 at 100&deg; to 120&deg;, a
    third to 85&nbsp;% of the area has its normal stress rising faster than
    its shear and can never fail (e), and half the area is not slipping by
    6&nbsp;&lambda;<sub>0</sub>. The same tensors at 150&deg; are the
    easiest in the sweep, 186 to 203&nbsp;MPa, two and a half times less than
    fault-normal thrust.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>The observed sense of slip picks the oblique thrust-like corner.</b>
    The fault is reverse with a left-lateral component. In this family that
    rake, 45&deg; to 75&deg;, appears only with the compressive axis at
    120&deg; to 150&deg; (d), and near the plate-convergence azimuth of about
    130&deg; only the thrust-like half of the family (r&nbsp;&le;&nbsp;0.5)
    brings half the fault to failure within the scan, at 377 to
    397&nbsp;MPa, with a mean rake of 58&deg; to 73&deg;. The tensors that
    both load the fault and slip it the right way are therefore the oblique,
    thrust-leaning ones, and the pure strike-slip tensor at the convergence
    azimuth clamps half of the fault shut.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>The strength profile shifts the numbers, not the ordering.</b> The
    lithostatic model, weak in its shallow rows, has 3 to 45&nbsp;% of the
    area slipping already at the uniform model&rsquo;s first failure and
    reaches half the area at loads up to 20&nbsp;% lower for the thrust-like
    tensors and within 5&nbsp;% for the strike-slip ones (c against b), with
    a smoother activation curve (f); every ranking of azimuth and regime is
    the same. The area the load drives into tension stays under 0.2&nbsp;%
    on the uniform model and under 1.2&nbsp;% on the lithostatic one at
    6&nbsp;&lambda;<sub>0</sub>, so the linear criterion is not being used
    where it is wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>The kernel does not matter here, and the slivers did not break the
    solve.</b> The classical dislocation and the mollified kernel put the
    half-active load within 1.2&nbsp;% of each other on every tensor
    (median ratio 0.999), the active fraction within 0.011 at every load,
    and the moment within 4&nbsp;%. The symmetric part of the classical
    operator is strongly indefinite on this mesh (smallest eigenvalue
    &minus;95 against &minus;1.1 for the mollified kernel, the slivers&rsquo;
    self-stiffness), and the projected Gauss&ndash;Seidel iteration still
    converged in all 1,820 solves without the active-set fallback. The
    slips themselves, tens of metres at three times the first failure, are
    what a static load of several hundred MPa produces on a frictional
    surface with no other faults to share it: read the maps for where slip
    goes, not for how much.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-this-is-not">What this is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Not a model of Taiwan: one fault, with none of the others and none
      of the plate interface, so there is no interaction and no shadow.</li>
    <li>Not a slip-rate model: the load is a static stress and the answer a
      static slip, with no time, no geodesy and no geologic rate fitted.</li>
    <li>Not an absolute stress: everything is quoted relative to the
      tensor&rsquo;s own first failure or in MPa of maximum shear, and the
      150&nbsp;MPa and the pore ratio are assumptions, not measurements.</li>
    <li>Not a converged mesh: the segments are used as supplied, at one
      resolution, with their slivers.</li>
    <li>Uniform friction and a fixed rake per element. Opening is not
      modelled; where the load drives the normal stress through zero the
      linear criterion is wrong and the manifest says where.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="reproducing-it">Reproducing it</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-sh">python studies/active_fractal/selfcheck.py -k taiwan
python studies/active_fractal/taiwan.py assemble --workers 8   # the folded matrix, cached
python studies/active_fractal/taiwan.py run                    # 70 tensors x 2 kernels x 13 loads
python studies/active_fractal/taiwan.py figure                 # website/src/figures/taiwan_sweep.png
python studies/active_fractal/taiwan.py payload                # website/public/data/taiwan_*</code></pre>

  <script>
    import { startTaiwan } from "../viz/taiwan";
    startTaiwan(document.getElementById("viewer")!, import.meta.env.BASE_URL);
  </script>
</Base>
